    "origin_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/5-non-domiciled-drivers-want-cdls-reinstated-as-battle-separately-moves-through-courts",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-26T15:53:58.684806+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/5-non-domiciled-drivers-want-cdls-reinstated-as-battle-separately-moves-through-courts",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/5-non-domiciled-drivers-want-cdls-reinstated-as-battle-separately-moves-through-courts",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-26T15:28:28+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-26T15:28:28+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-26T15:55:56.945045+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-26T15:55:56.945045+00:00",
    "body": "A simultaneous request by five truck drivers to have their CDLs reinstated after losing them through the change in federal policy regarding non-domiciled drivers is open for comments in the Federal Register, and already has attracted both support and pushback on the possibility.\nThe request by the five, published in Tuesday’s Federal Register , comes as the industry awaits a court ruling that could overturn the non-domiciled rule implemented earlier this year , with a federal court already having shot down one interpretation of the new policy.\nThe new non-domiciled rule, published in March, requires the states that issue CDLs to not issue them “unless the applicant provides evidence of lawful immigration status,” according to the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA) in recapping the background of the five driver requests.\nEach of the five drivers has a different set of details surrounding why they no longer have a CDL under the non-domiciled rule. But they all boil down to their respective ongoing applications seeking to turn a temporary legal situation into a more advanced status not having been completed, resulting in their licenses having been yanked or not extended due to their respective states’ interpretation of the rule.\nTelling their stories\nFor example, applicant Yehonatan Kapach , according to the Federal Register notice devoted to his request, holds an unexpired Employment Authorization Document (EAD), which was issued to him while he has a pending asylum application to remain in the U.S.\nHis CDL came from North Carolina, where he resides. It was due to expire in July 2028.\nBut in August, according to the notice, “the North Carolina DMV downgraded Mr. Kapach’s Class A CDL to a class C CDL. He states that the DMV did not offer any explanation of the legal basis for doing so in writing; rather, he was told orally that this was based on his lack of lawful permanent resident status.”\nKapach is asking for a 5-year exemption from 49 CFR 383.71(b)(9), the relevant federal statute, to allow North Carolina to reinstate his CDL.\nThe other four applicants all have similar stories. One of them, Nikolai Kostarev , in the country legally under asylum laws, is waiting for a green card, but his CDL from Oregon expired in August and he can not get a new one. “Mr. Kostarev indicates that there is no basis in his record to treat him as an unvetted, foreign-domiciled, new driver,” the application says.\nNick Castello is a disabled U.S. Marine Corps veteran. His request is more broadly made for FMCSA to offer relief to those who are in the U.S. legally but not of such a duration that they can get a CDL under the non-domiciled rule. There is little information in the request about his own situation.\nLuca Donati had his New Jersey CDL downgraded in February because his legal authorization expires in 2027. He is an Italian citizen in the U.S. lawfully.\nAmiran Morchiladze’s application is short on details about his background. But it does say he had his CDL from an unidentified state pulled in August while his residency status is going through the legal process, and “that without relief he may lose his truck, the small business he has been building, and his primary source of income.”\nAsking for your opinion\nThe comments window that was opened in response to the requests found some contributors submitting identical views on all five of the applicants. The window remains open until December 24.\nBut what it also showed was that within 48 hours, the applicants had a lot of supporters. They also had a lot of critics who did not believe they should receive any sort of dispensation.\nA comment from Coyote Creek Transportation, with the name of the submitter not identified, raised an issue that has always accompanied the new rule: how do you know the prior driving record of a non-domiciled applicant?\nIn the comments for the Kapach request, Coyote Creek said “FMCSA established specific eligibility and documentation requirements for non-domiciled CDL holders. Those requirements should not be bypassed through individual exemptions without compelling evidence that the same level of safety and regulatory compliance can be maintained.”\n“I respectfully request that FMCSA deny this exemption and consistently enforce the federal licensing requirements established for all commercial motor vehicle operators,” Coyote Creek said.\nCoyote Creek made similar comments across four of the five applicants.\nIn two of them, the commenter added: “The inability to independently verify an applicant’s prior driving history is a significant issue that must not be overlooked.”\n‘I followed the rules’\nDriver Dirk Van der Spek commented that he opposed all five requests.\nHe is a Dutch immigrant who said in his comment that he waited five years for a green card, and drove trucks for more than 20 years, mostly for C.R. England.\n“I did it by following the rules, even when that was slow and hard,” Van der Spek wrote.\nThe amended version of the non-domiciled CDL, Van der Spek said, “finally enforces requirements that were on the books for years, and it has been in effect for only eight months. Granting exemptions now, one driver at a time or for whole immigration categories, would start walking it back before it has had a chance to work.”\nThose who were impacted tell their stories\nThe other side of the argument often came from people who had been behind the wheel but no longer can be as a result of the rule.\nIn a post in broken English, Harpreet Singh said in commenting on the Donati application that “I was driving truck almost 8 years. This administration canceled my license.”\n“My work authorization and license get expired same year and same month but they still canceled it,” Singh wrote. “now I get my C class license and do Uber try you feed my family.”\nDonati received similar support in a comment from Tendresse Miafouna (gender not disclosed but Tendresse is generally a female name) who said she was in the same situation as Donati.\n“I have Granted Asylum status, and my application for adjustment of status is in process,” Miafouna said. “I have lived in Texas for 9 yrs. My Class A CDL was not expired it was valid until 07/2031 because my I-94 status is “Granted Indefinitely.” Despite this my CDL was downgraded to Class C. I have no tickets, no violations or accidents and never been placed under out-of-service orders.”\nWhat will the courts do?\nThe irony of the five applications is that they are dropping as the non-domiciled rule’s fate is awaiting a ruling in the case of Lujan vs. FMCSA, which was a multi-plaintiff lawsuit aimed at overturning the FMCSA regulation in the Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia. Lead plaintiff Jorge Rivera Lujan is in the U.S. legally under DACA status.\nSo far, the fight against the rule has mostly gone in favor of the rule’s opponents.\nIn the Lujan case, oral arguments have taken place in the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia. In those oral arguments, questioning from the bench appeared to be tougher on the FMCSA attorneys than those looking to overturn the rule.\nEarlier this month, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit backed the state of New York in its fight to reinstate the loss of some federal funding that was withheld over the state’s issuance of non-domiciled CDLs for a holder whose authorization to stay in the U.S expired before the CDL’s tenure ends.\nThat decision did not overturn the non-domiciled rule, just the denial of federal funds over the state’s policy. But it could be seen as a court finding fault with some aspects of the non-domiciled rule.\nOn November 13, the day it was handed down, attorneys fighting FMCSA in two courtrooms over the non-domiciled rule quickly noted to their respective judges what had occurred.\nFor example, attorneys for California immediately wrote to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia to notify it of the New York decision, as California is fighting the Department of Transportation and FMCSA over the same issue: denial of funds over the state’s non-domiciled policies.\n“FMCSA has asserted the same erroneous interpretation of the relevant regulations here, which fails for the same reasons,” California Deputy Attorney General Kristen Kido said in her letter.\nAnd three days after the New York ruling, the plaintiffs in Lujan, which also include the American Federation of State, County & Municipal Employees, and American Federation of Teachers, also wrote to the same court (where their case is being heard) to notify it of the New York decision.\n“The Second Circuit’s unanimous holding (in the New York case) that previous CDL regulations\ncontained no date-matching requirement supports Petitioners’ argument that FMCSA’s professed concern about state noncompliance with such a requirement does not support the Final Rule,” the letter said.\nMore articles by John Kingston\nSCOTUS action revives shipper/receiver liability post-Montgomery\nUber Freight makes a very public break with Gartner’s Quadrant\nC.H. Robinson: no evidence of racketeering, wants RICO case moved",
